    {"principle": "Perforated viscus typically presents with board-like abdominal rigidity, and missed air on imaging; it is not usually associated with maroon stools alone.", "source": "General surgery"}
  ],
  "checks": [
    {"check": "Does the solver recognize that the patient's atrial fibrillation and recent missed dabigatran doses increase thromboembolic risk?", "status": "pass", "note": "This is key to evaluating embolic causes like cardiac or paradoxical thromboembolism."},
    {"check": "Does the solver note that maroon-colored stools suggest lower gastrointestinal bleeding from ischemic colitis or mucosal necrosis?", "status": "pass", "note": "Maroon stools are consistent with ischemic colitis but not typical of perforation."},
    {"check": "Does the solver consider that abdominal tenderness in all quadrants with distention and absent bowel sounds suggests peritonitis from ischemic colitis rather than perforated herniation?", "status": "pass", "note": "Perforated herniation usually localizes tenderness to the hernial defect."},
